# The target list

Written on build day, before anybody leaves the room.

This is the step that gets skipped because the room is tired, and it is the one defect
that undid a whole client build. A principal on that build said out loud, on a recorded
call, that without a target list and a deadline she would never find the time to start.
She was right, and nobody had given her one.

## Why a date rather than an intention

"Go and play with it when you can" loses to email every single time. A named thing, by a
named date, with a named person checking, is what turns a working install into a used
one.

## Week one

| Who | What they will do | By when | Who is checking |
|---|---|---|---|
| [name] | [one specific task, from their own three-jobs answer] | [date] | [name] |

One target each. Not three. Somebody who hits one target comes back for the next one.

## The cadence after that

A skill a week is the default, because a client asked for exactly that. Adjust it to the
team rather than defending the number.

| When | What | Who |
|---|---|---|
| Weekly | [The skill of the week, or the target] | [Champion] |
| Weekly | 30 minute drop-in, same time each week | [Champion] |
| Monthly | Check-in with [IgniteAI contact] | [Champion] |
| Quarterly | Review what is installed, what gets used, what has gone stale | [Owner] |

## The champion

[Name.] Chosen because they are already getting something out of it, they are willing to
build, and they are trusted enough to be told the truth. Seniority is not one of the
three.

What they get: permission to create skills, a named channel, a 30 minute orientation,
and recognition that reaches whoever runs their appraisal.

What they own: two or three starter skills in month one, one workshop where everybody
present builds something for a real task, the team's plugin, and an honest report of
what is stuck.

What is not theirs: policy, spend, or blame. Anything touching client data, money, or
leaving the building goes up rather than sideways.

Honest time cost: three to four hours in month one, then one to two hours a month. If it
is costing more than that, something upstream is wrong.

## The next check-in

[Date, in the diary, with an invitation sent. Not "we will sort something out".]

## What gets measured, and when

At day 30, walk the task inventory again and re-ask the two time columns. Report hours
saved per week with the assumption visible, and say which rows were measured and which
were estimated.

Report a named result per person rather than a usage count. "[Name] now does the Monday
report in 10 minutes instead of 90" lands. "Adoption is up" does not.

